{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/AlecKriebel/Math/pulls/261","id":4703452619,"node_id":"PR_kwDOTfIvP88AAAABGFj9yw","html_url":"https://github.com/AlecKriebel/Math/pull/261","diff_url":"https://github.com/AlecKriebel/Math/pull/261.diff","patch_url":"https://github.com/AlecKriebel/Math/pull/261.patch","issue_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/AlecKriebel/Math/issues/261","commits_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/AlecKriebel/Math/pulls/261/commits","review_comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/AlecKriebel/Math/pulls/261/comments","review_comment_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/AlecKriebel/Math/pulls/comments{/number}","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/AlecKriebel/Math/issues/261/comments","statuses_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/AlecKriebel/Math/statuses/7b7fcd63d5ea83df3a4c29853c4ba34b93d16f07","number":261,"state":"open","locked":false,"title":"5100021: prove the focal pedal–antipedal area product (k403,b)","user":{"login":"AlecKriebel","id":6109370,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjYxMDkzNzA=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/6109370?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/AlecKriebel","html_url":"https://github.com/AlecKriebel","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/AlecKriebel/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/AlecKriebel/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/AlecKriebel/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/AlecKriebel/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/AlecKriebel/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/AlecKriebel/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/AlecKriebel/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/AlecKriebel/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/AlecKriebel/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"## Result\n\nClaimed solved after one substantive author turn, with a separate full adversarial audit. The exact Table 5 target is the signed area of the pedal of the original billiard orbit times the signed area of its original antipedal, taken from either original focus. The product is constant along each primitive orbit family with period divisible by four and a strictly nested nondegenerate confocal elliptical caustic. Primitive star windings are included.\n\nAll real antipedal vertices are finite. The focal pedal area is strictly positive in the chosen orientation; the antipedal area may vanish. This is a product theorem, and zero products are allowed without dividing by a zero area. The origin/odd-period k403,a row and the parallel k404 ratio are different targets.\n\n## Proof and credit\n\nThe proof derives the actual focal line intersections and side projections, proves cancellation of the potential double poles including period four, identifies complementary cyclic dn traces, and establishes their half-real-period product identity on the reduced elliptic torus. Direct symbolic four-period geometry is included.\n\nClassical Jacobi parametrization and compact-torus methods are credited. The overlapping even-period antipedal lemma was cross-checked with the parallel k404 author; SHARED_PROVENANCE.md records this exchange. That author was not the independent reviewer. No historical novelty, human peer review, or proposer acceptance is claimed.\n\n## Independent review and checks\n\nThe separate reviewer returned PASS_COMPLETE_SOURCE_TARGET with no mandatory corrections. Its nine portable files are preserved unchanged in independent_review/. The audit independently reconstructed the line geometry, pole cancellation, trace divisor, parity shift and real-domain claims.\n\n- Frozen proof SHA-256: f7aada9e28e92c6fab920f1330366acd6eaba8006b4cd0ce52e2dbd58e7ef5b9\n- Author manifest SHA-256: 3bc00c10fb0af01b733c05edb896f7e74e617796a79b6ec4486929e64c361596\n- Independent review SHA-256: c7cc49d934eca5090e17d70c821552fd1e995f3ae1f25f645caf868cb4361630\n- Author controls: 12,029 exact; 10,284 separately labeled 95-digit diagnostics, both replayed byte-for-byte\n- Independent controls: 6,469 exact; 14,932 separately labeled 110-digit diagnostics, including physical ray-traced orbits and complex near-pole checks\n\nThe numerical diagnostics are not interval proofs; the universal theorem is established by the written argument and its independent mathematical audit. Historical author status files remain frozen, and this review records the subsequent disposition.\n\n## Queue and publication scope\n\nOnly problem 5100021's QUEUE row is changed to claimed_solved, 1/5, with the source/domain qualifications. This is one draft PR for the problem. Source PDFs, imported records and exploratory files are excluded; main is not directly modified.","labels":[],"milestone":null,"active_lock_reason":null,"created_at":"2026-10-01T12:12:30Z","updated_at":"2026-10-01T12:13:49Z","closed_at":null,"merged_at":null,"merge_commit_sha":"0ffda03d77a69d46f7d1c882a84dc92f2e711b17","assignee":null,"assignees":[],"requested_reviewers":[],"requested_teams":[],"head":{"label":"AlecKriebel:dot/math-5100021","ref":"dot/math-5100021","repo":{"id":1307717439,"node_id":"R_kgDOTfIvPw","name":"Math","full_name":"AlecKriebel/Math","owner":{"login":"AlecKriebel","id":6109370,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjYxMDkzNzA=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/6109370?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/AlecKriebel","html_url":"https://github.com/AlecKriebel","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/AlecKriebel/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/AlecKriebel/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/AlecKriebel/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/AlecKriebel/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/AlecKriebel/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/AlecKriebel/orgs","repos_url":"https://